<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">To use after class or in groups. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>Name at least four names of the sacrament of Reconciliation and explain what each one emphasizes. Which name resonates most with you personally, and why?</li>
<li>In which biblical passage does Christ institute this sacrament? Cite the key verse and describe the context: where were the disciples, what time was it, what was the atmosphere?</li>
<li>List the three acts of the penitent and explain each one in two lines. Then, for each act, identify what part of the prodigal son's story it corresponds to.</li>
<li>What is the difference between perfect contrition and imperfect contrition? Which of the two suffices for a valid confession? Why is this distinction pastorally important?</li>
<li>What does it mean that confession must be "integral"? How is a mortal sin confessed? What would happen if you deliberately omitted a mortal sin?</li>
<li>What is the sacramental seal? Is there any exception? Name a saint who died to protect it.</li>
<li>What are the effects of the sacrament of Reconciliation? Name at least four, and for each one write one sentence about what it means in practice.</li>
<li>What is an indulgence and what is required to gain a plenary indulgence? Explain the difference between guilt and temporal punishment.</li>
<li>What does <em>in persona Christi</em> mean, and why is it crucial for understanding this sacrament?</li>
<li>Why does the Church recommend frequent confession even of venial sins? Give at least three reasons.</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question with total honesty. Write by hand if you can. The act of writing slows down the mind and lets the truth surface.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. When was your last confession? If it was more than six months ago, what has stopped you from going back: shame, laziness, fear, mistrust of the priest, the feeling that you have no serious sins?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Be honest with yourself. Identifying the obstacle is the first step to overcoming it. Write the obstacle here. Don't judge it; just name it.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. In the parable of the prodigal son, the father runs toward the son before the son even finishes speaking. What does that tell you about what God is like? Have you ever experienced God's forgiveness in a way that felt like someone running toward you?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Describe, if you can, a concrete moment when you felt God's unconditional forgiveness. If you can't think of one, describe what you imagine it would feel like. Sometimes imagining it is the first step to experiencing it.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. Is there any sin you have never confessed, or that you confessed incompletely, out of shame? Write here what kind it is (you don't need to detail it, just the category: against purity, against truth, against charity, etc.).</p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="question-block__p">What would happen if you confessed it? What's the worst that could actually happen? (The priest might pause. That's it. He's heard worse. And he cannot tell anyone. Ever.)</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. What is harder for you: asking for forgiveness or forgiving? Explain why. Be concrete: is there a specific situation or person that makes one of these especially difficult?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Many people discover that forgiving someone who hurt them is even harder than recognizing their own mistakes. Your own sins are inside you; the other person's sin against you is outside, and you can't control it. That's why forgiving feels like losing control. But it's actually gaining freedom.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. Write the name of a person you need to forgive. Then write why you haven't forgiven them yet. What would you lose if you forgave them? What would you gain?</p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Sometimes we don't forgive because the resentment has become part of our identity. "I am the one who was wronged by X." Letting go of the resentment feels like losing yourself. But the self built on resentment is a prison. Who would you be without this grudge?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">6. If you knew with absolute certainty that the priest does not judge you, that he forgets your sins immediately, and that he is utterly incapable of telling anyone under any circumstances &mdash;would anything change in how you confess? Would you confess things you have been hiding?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Sometimes it's not faith that fails; it's human trust. The seal of confession is not a theological abstraction. It is a concrete protection backed by the harshest penalty in Church law and by the blood of martyrs. Write what you would say if you truly believed no one else would ever know.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Guided Examination of Conscience</h4>
<p>Review the Ten Commandments. Next to each one, write whether you have kept it well, moderately, or poorly in the last month. For those you mark "poorly," jot down briefly what happened. You don't have to show this to anyone; it's for you. But it will help you in your next confession.</p>
<ol>
<li>Love God above all things: ___________</li>
<li>Do not take God's name in vain: ___________</li>
<li>Keep holy the Lord's Day: ___________</li>
<li>Honor your father and mother: ___________</li>
<li>Do not kill (includes anger, grudges, harsh words): ___________</li>
<li>Do not commit impure acts: ___________</li>
<li>Do not steal: ___________</li>
<li>Do not lie or bear false witness: ___________</li>
<li>Do not consent to impure thoughts or desires: ___________</li>
<li>Do not covet the goods of others: ___________</li>
</ol>
<p>Now add: Have I neglected prayer? Have I been ungrateful? Have I been prideful? Have I envied someone? Am I too attached to comfort, money, or pleasure? The examination is not a test you pass or fail; it is a mirror. Look honestly.</p>

<h4>B. Preparing Your Next Confession</h4>
<p>Write on a separate sheet (that you can bring to the confessional):</p>
<ol>
<li>How long it has been since your last confession.</li>
<li>Mortal sins in kind and approximate number.</li>
<li>The venial sins that are most repeated (the "favorite" sins, the ones you keep falling into).</li>
<li>Any circumstance that changes the species of the sin (if any, briefly).</li>
<li>Something for which you want to thank God (there's always something &mdash;even if it's just "I'm still alive, I'm here, I haven't given up").</li>
<li>One near occasion of sin you will avoid this week.</li>
</ol>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>C. Meditative Reading of the Parable of the Prodigal Son</h4>
<p>Read Luke 15:11-32 slowly, out loud if possible. Now underline or note down every action the father performs. Count them. How many acts of love did you find? Compare: how many words does the son say and how many actions does the father do? What does that tell you about how God's love works?</p>
<p>Now notice what the father does NOT do: he does not demand an explanation. He does not lecture. He does not impose conditions. He does not say "I told you so." He does not make the son earn his way back. He runs, embraces, clothes, and feasts. God's mercy is not earned; it is received.</p>

<h4>D. Rembrandt's Father's Hands</h4>
<p>Find Rembrandt's <em>The Return of the Prodigal Son</em>. Study it closely for at least five minutes. The father's hands are different: one is more masculine and strong (like a father's hand), the other is more delicate and fine (like a mother's hand). Rembrandt meant that God loves us with both fatherly and motherly love.</p>
<p>Now look at the son's feet: one sandal is broken, the other gone. His head is shaved like a prisoner. These are the marks of someone who has hit absolute bottom. The father's hands rest on those marks without flinching.</p>
<p>Look at the elder brother: illuminated but distant. His hands are crossed &mdash;neither reaching out nor receiving. He is present but absent. He has done everything right and cannot enter the feast.</p>
<p>Write down: Where are you in this painting? Are you the son receiving the embrace? The elder brother watching with resentment? The father learning to love? Somewhere in between all three? Be honest. There is no wrong answer, only an honest one or a dishonest one.</p>

<h4>E. Your Personal Act of Contrition</h4>
<p>Compose your own Act of Contrition in your own words. Don't copy the traditional formula. Make it yours. Start: "Jesus, I am sorry for... because..." It can be brief. But let it be sincere. Speak to God as you would speak to someone who already knows everything and loves you anyway &mdash;because that's exactly what God is.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>F. Reconciliation Commitment</h4>
<p>Write a concrete commitment for this week. Be specific. Vague commitments produce vague results. "I will try to be better" means nothing. "I will go to confession at St. Mary's at 4pm on Saturday" means everything.</p>
<ul>
<li>I will go to confession. Date: ___________ Church/time: ___________</li>
<li>I will forgive ___________ with a concrete gesture: ___________ (e.g., "I will send a message," "I will pray for them by name every day," "I will stop avoiding them at family gatherings")</li>
<li>I will ask forgiveness from ___________ for ___________ (e.g., "I will apologize to my sister for snapping at her," "I will admit to my coworker that I took credit for her work")</li>
<li>One near occasion of sin I will avoid this week: ___________</li>
<li>One positive habit I will build in the space that sin occupied: ___________ (Nature abhors a vacuum. If you remove a sin without filling the space with something good, the sin will return with seven friends &mdash;see Lk 11:24-26.)</li>
</ul>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Verse to Memorize</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>&laquo;Receive the Holy Spirit.<br />
If you forgive the sins of any,<br />
they are forgiven;<br />
if you retain the sins of any,<br />
they are retained.&raquo;</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; John 20:22-23</p>
</div>

<p>Also commit this to memory:</p>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>&laquo;If we confess our sins,<br />
he who is faithful and just<br />
will forgive us our sins<br />
and cleanse us from all unrighteousness.&raquo;</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; 1 John 1:9</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for This Week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus, Good Shepherd,<br />
who said to the paralytic: "Your sins are forgiven,"<br />
and to the good thief: "Today you will be with me in Paradise,"<br />
and to the woman caught in adultery: "Neither do I condemn you; go and sin no more":<br />
give me the humility to acknowledge my sins without excuses,<br />
the courage to confess them without hiding anything,<br />
and the gratitude to live in your forgiveness<br />
without falling back into guilt.<br />
Give me also a heart that knows how to forgive<br />
as you forgive me:<br />
without conditions, without keeping score,<br />
with the joy of the father who runs to meet his son,<br />
with the tenderness of the hands that rest on the prodigal's shoulders,<br />
with the feast that celebrates the return of the lost.<br />
May I never forget that your mercy<br />
is infinitely greater than all my sins,<br />
and that the confessional is not a courtroom<br />
but the threshold of my Father's house.<br />
St. John Vianney, pray for me.<br />
St. Padre Pio, pray for me.<br />
St. Faustina, pray for me.<br />
St. John Nepomucene, protector of the seal, pray for me.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Examination of Conscience for Daily Use</h3>

<p>A short form for the end of each day. Takes two minutes. Changes everything:</p>

<ol>
<li>Place yourself in God's presence. Thank him for one specific thing from today.</li>
<li>Ask the Holy Spirit for light: "Show me what you see."</li>
<li>Review the day: What did I do well? Where did I fail? What did I avoid doing that I should have done?</li>
<li>What was my dominant thought or mood today? Was it loving, anxious, resentful, grateful, distracted?</li>
<li>Did I hurt anyone with words, actions, or omissions?</li>
<li>Did I neglect prayer or rush through it?</li>
<li>Ask forgiveness for the failings you see. Make a brief act of contrition.</li>
<li>Make one concrete resolution for tomorrow. Just one. Small and specific.</li>
</ol>

<p>This nightly examen is not confession, but it prepares you for confession. It keeps the conscience awake. It prevents the buildup of unexamined sins. Many saints practiced it daily; few reached holiness without it.</p>

</section>

